# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the prebuilt ONNX Runtime.
#
# laya-cpp links the official ONNX Runtime binary that cmake/onnxruntime.cmake downloads and
# sha256-verifies. That library is not built with -fsanitize=thread, so TSan cannot see its
# internal synchronization: the happens-before edges its own mutexes and atomics establish are
# invisible, and every buffer its intra-op thread pool hands back to the main thread therefore
# looks like an unsynchronized access.
#
# Measured, not assumed: the `onnx (clang++, tsan)` job reported 342 races, every single one with
# a libonnxruntime.so frame, and all of them in `memcpy` or `operator delete`. 20 of 479 tests
# failed as a result, including four that only assert the static-holder helper's exit status and
# saw TSan's own exitcode=66.
#
# Scope, and what it costs. `called_from_lib` drops a report only when the accessing stack runs
# inside this library, so a race between two laya threads on laya-owned state is still reported.
# What it does give up is a race where laya hands memory to an ONNX Runtime worker thread, since
# that thread's stack is inside the library. TSan cannot adjudicate those either way while the
# library is uninstrumented, so the choice is between suppressing them and switching the whole
# cell off; this keeps the laya-vs-laya coverage the cell exists for, which is what
# OnnxRig.ConcurrentPredictAgreesWithASerialCall and CloseInterruptsARunInFlight exercise.
#
# Revisit if ONNX Runtime ever ships a TSan-instrumented build, or if laya starts passing a
# callback into the library.
called_from_lib:libonnxruntime.so
